#. translator: "# " starts a heading, a blank line starts a paragraph,
#. * and the "**bold**" / "*italic*" markers must stay in balanced
#. * pairs.
#: src/puzzle-info-text.h:32
msgid ""
"An **Acrostic** is a quote-based word puzzle.\n"
"\n"
"Acrostics consists of two parts: a grid containing a **Quote**, and a list "
"of clues with answers. Each cell in the grid has a label and corresponds to "
"one of the cells in an answer. The puzzle can be solved by a combination of "
"guessing words in the quote and solving the clues.\n"
"\n"
"# Quote\n"
"The quote is hidden in the grid and its letters are what every answer is "
"spelled from.\n"
"\n"
"# Author / Title\n"
"The first letter of each answer will typically spell out the **Author** and/"
"or **Title** of the work the quote came from. It can only use letters that "
"appear in the quote itself."
msgstr ""

#. translator: "# " starts a heading, a blank line starts a paragraph,
#. * and the "**bold**" / "*italic*" markers must stay in balanced
#. * pairs.
#: src/puzzle-info-text.h:53
msgid ""
"A **Barred crossword** uses thick bars along the edges of cells to mark "
"where words end. Every square in the grid holds a letter, and most belong to "
"both an across and a down clue. Grids normally show quarter symmetry, though "
"there are many variants.\n"
"\n"
"# Filling the Grid\n"
"With no blocks to break up the grid, a barred crossword is more tightly "
"constrained than other forms. Filling these puzzles often require obscure "
"words. As a result, they are among the hardest puzzles to set — and to "
"solve.\n"
"\n"
"# Clues and Preamble\n"
"Barred puzzles usually take **Cryptic-style** clues, where each clue "
"combines a definition with some wordplay leading to the same answer. They "
"sometimes feature a **Preamble** setting out a rule the solver must apply, "
"such as omitting a letter from every answer before entering it."
msgstr ""

#. translator: "# " starts a heading, a blank line starts a paragraph,
#. * and the "**bold**" / "*italic*" markers must stay in balanced
#. * pairs.
#: src/puzzle-info-text.h:75
msgid ""
"This section holds text fields visible to users. Every field here is\n"
"optional. A few clarifications:\n"
"\n"
"# Introduction\n"
"This is shown above the grid. It shows a message from the setter, or\n"
"perhaps a little bit of context. It's generally short.\n"
"\n"
"# Notes\n"
"A longer free-form field. Visually it appears just below the grid. This\n"
"is where longer instructions are spelled out for a themed or variant\n"
"puzzle.\n"
"\n"
"# Difficulty\n"
"This is advisory and free-form. Suggested values are words like\n"
"*\"Easy\"*, *\"Medium\"*, or *\"Difficult\"*.\n"
"\n"
"# Explanation\n"
"A brief message presented to the user after they solve the puzzle. It is\n"
"intended to give context on the solution."
msgstr ""

#. translator: "# " starts a heading, a blank line starts a paragraph,
#. * and the "**bold**" / "*italic*" markers must stay in balanced
#. * pairs.
#: src/puzzle-info-text.h:99
msgid ""
"These settings describe the conventions a solver should expect.\n"
"\n"
"# Show Enumerations\n"
"**Enumerations** are a hint about the length of the answer after the\n"
"clue, such as *(3,4)* for a two-word answer. They are common in\n"
"British-style and Cryptic puzzles.\n"
"\n"
"# Country and Language\n"
"These fields records the **country** the puzzle is associated\n"
"with and the **language** it is written in. Together, they determine\n"
"the alphabet for the puzzle, and valid letters to be entered in the\n"
"grid."
msgstr ""

#. translator: "# " starts a heading, a blank line starts a paragraph,
#. * and the "**bold**" / "*italic*" markers must stay in balanced
#. * pairs.
#: src/puzzle-info-text.h:116
msgid ""
"This section records who made the puzzle and where it appeared.\n"
"Every field here is optional.\n"
"\n"
"# Credits\n"
"**Author** is whoever created the puzzle and **Editor** whoever\n"
"edited it for publication. **Copyright** is a short notice, such\n"
"as *\"© 2026 Example, Inc\"*.\n"
"\n"
"# Publication\n"
"**Publisher** names who published the puzzle, while\n"
"**Publication** is a fuller bibliographic reference to where it\n"
"appeared. **Date** is the date of publication. **Puzzle URL**\n"
"should be a permanent link."
msgstr ""

